window.CARDS=(window.CARDS||[]).concat([{"t":"Kaizen","d":"Japanese word for continuous improvement","g":4,"p":"Kaizen means continuous improvement through frequent, practical changes involving the people who do the work.","e":"A team holds a short improvement event to reduce unnecessary movement in a work area.","m":"Change for the better.","x":"Kaizen is not limited to large projects or executive-led transformation.","r":["Continuous and Disruptive Change | Disruptive Changes | Continuous Improvement Principles"],"w":"For Kaizen, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Continuous and Disruptive Change","d":"Continuous changes are mostly incremental. They do not necessarily eliminate major costs, but when taken together, small changes can add up to big savings.","g":4,"p":"Continuous change improves an existing system in smaller steps. Disruptive change replaces major assumptions, technology, or ways of working.","e":"Example: Before changing sample processing, leaders at a laboratory explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Continuous and Disruptive Change: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Continuous and Disruptive Change, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Change Management | Continuous Improvement Principles | Disruptive Changes"],"w":"For Continuous and Disruptive Change, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Disruptive Changes","d":"Differ from continuous changes in that they are less frequent, more costly, and may have a significant impact on the way in which work is accomplished.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Disruptive Changes means differ from continuous changes in that they are less frequent, more costly, and may have a significant impact on the way in which work is accomplished.","e":"Example: Before changing ticket resolution, leaders at a customer-support center explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Disruptive Changes: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Disruptive Changes, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Continuous and Disruptive Change | Kaizen | Continuous Improvement Principles"],"w":"For Disruptive Changes, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Continuous Improvement Principles","d":"Continuous improvement is supported by strategic alignment, leadership approval, an efficiency-focused culture, visible success stories, formal structure, measurement, and employee participation.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Continuous Improvement Principles means continuous improvement is supported by strategic alignment, leadership approval, an efficiency-focused culture, visible success stories, formal structure, measurement, and employee participation.","e":"A team applies Continuous Improvement Principles to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"Continuous Improvement Principles: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Continuous Improvement Principles, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories | CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency | CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"For Continuous Improvement Principles, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan","d":"To ensure that the continuous improvement of the organization’s processes and activities are central to is sustainability, Continuous Improvement must be incorporated into the strategic plan.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan means to ensure that the continuous improvement of the organization’s processes and activities are central to is sustainability, Continuous Improvement must be incorporated into the strategic plan.","e":"A team applies CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"CI lasts only when incorporate in strategic plan becomes part of normal work.","x":"For CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories | CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency | CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"For CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Top Management Approval","d":"Unless top management believes that Continuous Improvement is important and unless they actively approve of a Continuous Improvement program, it is unlikely those below them in the hierarchy will give it much attention","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, CI Principles: Top Management Approval means unless top management believes that Continuous Improvement is important and unless they actively approve of a Continuous Improvement program, it is unlikely those below them in the hierarchy will give it much attention","e":"A team applies CI Principles: Top Management Approval to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"CI lasts only when top management approval becomes part of normal work.","x":"For CI Principles: Top Management Approval, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories | CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency | CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"For CI Principles: Top Management Approval, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","d":"The concept of efficiency and improvement must be an integral part of the organizations culture. It must be part of its DNA.X","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency means concept of efficiency and improvement must be an integral part of the organizations culture. It must be part of its DNA.","e":"Leaders apply CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency while introducing a new workflow, addressing concerns, building support, and reinforcing adoption.","m":"CI lasts only when culture of efficiency becomes part of normal work.","x":"For CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories | CI Principles: Formal Structure | CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan"],"w":"For CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"CI Principles: Create Success Stories","d":"Successes cannot be kept quite. They must be celebrated and shared.X","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, CI Principles: Create Success Stories means successes cannot be kept quite. They must be celebrated and shared.","e":"A team applies CI Principles: Create Success Stories to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"CI lasts only when create success stories becomes part of normal work.","x":"For CI Principles: Create Success Stories, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency | CI Principles: Formal Structure | CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan"],"w":"For CI Principles: Create Success Stories, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Formal Structure","d":"Many organizations have found that it is necessary to create a formal structure that promotes Continuous Improvement in the organization.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, CI Principles: Formal Structure means many organizations have found that it is necessary to create a formal structure that promotes Continuous Improvement in the organization.","e":"A team applies CI Principles: Formal Structure to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"CI lasts only when formal structure becomes part of normal work.","x":"For CI Principles: Formal Structure, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories | CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency | CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan"],"w":"For CI Principles: Formal Structure, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Measurement","d":"Continuous Improvement projects must measure process outcomes. In this process they collect data and compare existing processes with new or improved ones. X","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, CI Principles: Measurement means continuous Improvement projects must measure process outcomes. In this process they collect data and compare existing processes with new or improved ones.","e":"A team applies CI Principles: Measurement while checking whether observed differences come from the process, the instrument, or the people taking measurements.","m":"CI lasts only when measurement becomes part of normal work.","x":"For CI Principles: Measurement, do not assume observed variation comes entirely from the process; the measurement system may contribute.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories | CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency | CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"For CI Principles: Measurement, this matters because reliable measurement is necessary before a team can distinguish real process change from measurement error."},{"t":"Eleven Steps to CI","d":"A structured continuous-improvement effort moves from selecting and defining a problem through measurement, mapping, analysis, redesign, implementation, and ongoing control.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps to CI means structured continuous-improvement effort moves from selecting and defining a problem through measurement, mapping, analysis, redesign, implementation, and ongoing control.","e":"Example: A pharmacy maps prescription filling from request to completion and discovers repeated handoffs, waiting, and unnecessary movement that were hidden in day-to-day work.","m":"Eleven Steps to CI: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps to CI, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark | Eleven Steps: Chart"],"w":"For Eleven Steps to CI, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities","d":"Opportunities must first be identified. What can help is a Gemba walk. Ideas need to be encouraged and welcomed. Since there are many possibilities, they need to be prioritized before selecting the ones that promise to generate the most benefit to the organization.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities means opportunities must first be identified. What can help is a Gemba walk. Ideas need to be encouraged and welcomed. Since there are many possibilities, they need to be prioritized before selecting the ones that promise to generate the most benefit to the...","e":"A team applies Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Define Project","d":"A clear and concise project definition is required so that all those involved understand its goals and objectives. But, equally important to focusing on the definition is the need to engage stakeholders at this stage in a Continuous Improvement project.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Define Project means clear and concise project definition is required so that all those involved understand its goals and objectives. But, equally important to focusing on the definition is the need to engage stakeholders at this stage in a Continuous Improvement project.","e":"A team applies Eleven Steps: Define Project to make work visible, limit congestion, and improve movement from request to completion.","m":"Eleven Steps: Define Project: Make work and bottlenecks visible.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Define Project, adding more work to the system can reduce flow when capacity is constrained.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Define Project, this matters because managing work flow reduces waiting, excess inventory, handoff delays, and unfinished work."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Describe Process","d":"Describe the Suppliers, Inputs, Process steps, Outcomes, and Customers. Flowcharts, such as a Value Stream Map, help to visualize flow through a process and clarify the steps and sequence of steps including any delays that may interfere with process throughput.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Describe Process means describe the Suppliers, Inputs, Process steps, Outcomes, and Customers. Flowcharts, such as a Value Stream Map, help to visualize flow through a process and clarify the steps and sequence of steps including any delays that may interfere with process...","e":"Example of Eleven Steps: Describe Process: A human-resources team maps employee onboarding from request to completion and discovers repeated handoffs, waiting, and unnecessary movement that were hidden in day-to-day work.","m":"Eleven Steps: Describe Process: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Describe Process, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Describe Process, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Measure","d":"Measurement in almost every processes improvement effort is absolutely necessary. It helps to understand how the process is currently performing, define how it will be improved, and is necessary to determine performance once the changes have been implemented. But there is a challenge in the measurement process. They must be both precise and accurate.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Measure means measurement in almost every process-improvement effort is absolutely necessary. It helps to understand how the process is currently performing, define how it will be improved, and is necessary to determine performance once the changes have been...","e":"Example: Before changing invoice processing, leaders at a finance team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eleven Steps: Measure: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Measure, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Measure, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Benchmark","d":"Benchmarks are reference points that help set goals and measure progress against these goals. Often benchmarks come from external sources such as competitors or the best practices in an industry.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Benchmark means benchmarks are reference points that help set goals and measure progress against these goals. Often benchmarks come from external sources such as competitors or the best practices in an industry.","e":"A team applies Eleven Steps: Benchmark to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"Eleven Steps: Benchmark: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Benchmark, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Chart"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Benchmark, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Analyze","d":"In this step the data and information that have been collected is shared with stakeholders and analyzed. An important point here is we must be sure that we have found the root cause of a problem and not its symptoms.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Analyze means in this step the data and information that have been collected is shared with stakeholders and analyzed. An important point here is we must be sure that we have found the root cause of a problem and not its symptoms.","e":"Example: A hospital clinic investigates recurring failures in patient check-in. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Eleven Steps: Analyze: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Analyze, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Benchmark | Eleven Steps: Chart"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Analyze, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Chart","d":"Charts are visual aids that help us make sense of data. They are quite useful in the Analysis phase. The most useful Charts include histograms, ordered histograms, and fishbone diagrams.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Chart means charts are visual aids that help us make sense of data. They are quite useful in the Analysis phase. The most useful Charts include histograms, ordered histograms, and fishbone diagrams.","e":"Example: A laboratory builds a fishbone diagram for errors in sample processing, grouping possible causes under people, methods, equipment, materials, measurement, and environment.","m":"Eleven Steps: Chart: Remember the question the visual answers, not only its appearance.","x":"Choose the chart or map based on the question being answered; similar visuals may serve different purposes.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Chart, this matters because a well-chosen visual makes patterns, priorities, sequence, and unusual behavior easier to recognize."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Design new System","d":"Once the problem has been defined, measurements taken, flow charts drawn and analyzed, the next step is to design the system. In most circumstances it will be well advised to engage those who will eventually use the system.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Design new System means once the problem has been defined, measurements taken, flow charts drawn and analyzed, the next step is to design the system. In most circumstances it will be well advised to engage those who will eventually use the system.","e":"A team applies Eleven Steps: Design new System to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"Eleven Steps: Design new System: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Design new System, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Design new System, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Huddle","d":"Very much like the Daily Scrum, the huddle is an opportunity for those engaged in the improvement process to share progress and plans.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Huddle means very much like the Daily Scrum, the huddle is an opportunity for those engaged in the improvement process to share progress and plans.","e":"A team applies Eleven Steps: Huddle to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"Eleven Steps: Huddle: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Huddle, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Huddle, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Implementation","d":"Once designed, the new process is implemented. Unless those affected by the process have been engaged in its design, resistance-to-change can prove to be a hurdle during the implementation process.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Implementation means once designed, the new process is implemented. Unless those affected by the process have been engaged in its design, resistance-to-change can prove to be a hurdle during the implementation process.","e":"Example: Before changing new-feature release, leaders at a software team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eleven Steps: Implementation: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Implementation, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Implementation, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Control","d":"A monitoring and control system must be established, complete with control charts, to ensure that the process meets and continues to meet design objectives.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eleven Steps: Control means monitoring and control system must be established, complete with control charts, to ensure that the process meets and continues to meet design objectives.","e":"Example: A manufacturer plots weekly assembly process results on a control chart. A point beyond the upper limit triggers an investigation into a special cause rather than an immediate redesign of the whole process.","m":"Eleven Steps: Control: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eleven Steps: Control, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI | Eleven Steps: Analyze | Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"For Eleven Steps: Control, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Change Management","d":"The skills associate with addressing the issues of resistance-to-change and managing the change process to achieve a successful outcome where those affected by the change do not actively engage in resistance to new ways of getting work done.","g":4,"p":"Change management is the structured work of preparing, supporting, and reinforcing people as a new process or system is introduced.","e":"Leaders explain the reason for a new workflow, involve employees, train users, remove barriers, and reinforce adoption.","m":"Prepare, involve, enable, reinforce.","x":"Communication alone is not change management.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | CI Principles: Top Management Approval | Continuous and Disruptive Change"],"w":"For Change Management, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps to Change Management","d":"Kotter’s change model builds urgency, forms a guiding coalition, creates and communicates a vision, empowers action, generates short-term wins, consolidates gains, and anchors change in culture.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps to Change Management means kotter’s change model builds urgency, forms a guiding coalition, creates and communicates a vision, empowers action, generates short-term wins, consolidates gains, and anchors change in culture.","e":"Example: Before changing meal preparation, leaders at a restaurant explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps to Change Management: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps to Change Management, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps | Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture"],"w":"For Eight Steps to Change Management, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Urgency","d":"Creating a sense of urgency helps to implement change. Research has found that without it, few will be motivated to participate in or accept change. Without urgency, there is no compelling reason why staff should accept the changes that are likely to disrupt the way they work and disrupt the pattern of interactions with others in the organization.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Urgency means creating a sense of urgency helps to implement change. Research has found that without it, few will be motivated to participate in or accept change. Without urgency, there is no compelling reason why staff should accept the changes that are likely to...","e":"Example: Before changing loan application, leaders at a bank explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Urgency: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps: Urgency, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Urgency, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Coalition","d":"Coalitions of stakeholders can also help underscore the need for change. The broader the coalition, the more momentum behind the change. But it cannot be just any group that supports the change. Rather, it must have the power, respect, and motivation to add credibility to the effort","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Coalition means coalitions of stakeholders can also help underscore the need for change. The broader the coalition, the more momentum behind the change. But it cannot be just any group that supports the change. Rather, it must have the power, respect, and motivation to...","e":"Example: Before changing baggage handling, leaders at a airline explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Coalition: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps: Coalition, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Communicate"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Coalition, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy","d":"Change initiatives must be driven by a strong and clear vision of what will change, and how it will improve performance and how it will prepare the organization for the future.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy means change initiatives must be driven by a strong and clear vision of what will change, and how it will improve performance and how it will prepare the organization for the future.","e":"Example: Before changing prescription filling, leaders at a pharmacy explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Communicate","d":"Changes must be communicated to all stakeholders. They must not only understand why the changes are necessary and what changes will be made, but also when they will take place and the procedures followed to implement them.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Communicate means changes must be communicated to all stakeholders. They must not only understand why the changes are necessary and what changes will be made, but also when they will take place and the procedures followed to implement them.","e":"Example: Before changing room turnover, leaders at a hotel explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Communicate: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eight Steps: Communicate, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Communicate, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Empower","d":"Empowerment occurs when those who are affected by a change are heard by those responsible for making the change. But it also requires that reasonable suggestions are considered during the change process, and some, not necessarily all, are approved.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Empower means empowerment occurs when those who are affected by a change are heard by those responsible for making the change. But it also requires that reasonable suggestions are considered during the change process, and some, not necessarily all, are approved.","e":"Example: Before changing package routing, leaders at a delivery company explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Empower: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps: Empower, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Empower, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Short Term Wins","d":"It is important for the leader to publicly recognize a series of short-term wins or accomplishments. Most importantly, these wins will serve as opportunities to celebrate progress. In addition, they will go a long way in helping to sustain enthusiasm as the change initiative moves forward.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Short Term Wins means it is important for the leader to publicly recognize a series of short-term wins or accomplishments. Most importantly, these wins will serve as opportunities to celebrate progress. In addition, they will go a long way in helping to sustain enthusiasm as...","e":"Example: Before changing employee onboarding, leaders at a human-resources team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Short Term Wins: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps: Short Term Wins, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins | Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Short Term Wins, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains","d":"As progress continues, it is often useful for the leader to consolidate the gains that have been made and share them with stakeholders. Often, these gains can be used to motivate even more gains.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains means as progress continues, it is often useful for the leader to consolidate the gains that have been made and share them with stakeholders. Often, these gains can be used to motivate even more gains.","e":"A team applies Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains to a defined process problem, gathers evidence, and uses the concept to choose and verify an improvement.","m":"Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","d":"Culture changes slowly, but it can be changed. When change initiatives are successfully completed, it is important for leaders to “anchor” those successes to the existing culture of the organization. By anchoring the success of these changes to culture, the values and beliefs of the organization become stronger and more widely accepted. Future changes are likely to receive more support, and resistance will fall.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture means culture changes slowly, but it can be changed. When change initiatives are successfully completed, it is important for leaders to “anchor” those successes to the existing culture of the organization. By anchoring the success of these changes to culture,...","e":"Example: Before changing purchase-order approval, leaders at a purchasing team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture, announcing or training on a change does not prove that the change has been adopted.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Coalition | Eight Steps: Communicate"],"w":"For Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Theory of Constraints","d":"The Theory of Constraints was created by Eliyahu Goldratt. In almost every situation there are constraints that prevent us from reaching our goals. The Theory of Constraints is an approach that helps us recognize and overcome them.","g":4,"p":"The Theory of Constraints focuses improvement on the constraint that limits the performance of the entire system.","e":"A laboratory discovers that one analyzer limits total throughput, so improvement efforts focus there first.","m":"The bottleneck governs the system.","x":"Do not optimize every step equally; focus first on the system constraint.","r":["Identify Systems Constraints | Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints | Elevate the System’s Constraints"],"w":"For Theory of Constraints, this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps","d":"The five focusing steps are identify the constraint, exploit it, subordinate other work to it, elevate it, and repeat when the constraint moves.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps means five focusing steps are identify the constraint, exploit it, subordinate other work to it, elevate it, and repeat when the constraint moves.","e":"Example: A laboratory finds that one approval step limits the total flow of sample processing. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps: Find the bottleneck, use it fully, align the system, then expand it.","x":"For Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps, improving a nonconstraint may increase local efficiency without increasing system throughput.","r":["Theory of Constraints | Theory of Constraints: Benefits | Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints"],"w":"For Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps, this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Identify Systems Constraints","d":"The first step is to identify the constraints in a process. They might be, delays, inadequate staff, slow machines, and manifest themselves as backups and bottlenecks. Value Stream maps at this stage can be very helpful. Another useful tool I the Kanban chart. The Eight Wastes may also impose constraints on process throughput.","g":4,"p":"Find the step that most limits the system's ability to achieve its goal. The constraint is the point where improvement has the greatest leverage.","e":"Example: A customer-support center uses To Do, Doing, and Done columns for ticket resolution and limits the Doing column to five items. New work is pulled only when capacity opens.","m":"Identify Systems Constraints: Find the bottleneck, use it fully, align the system, then expand it.","x":"For Identify Systems Constraints, improving a nonconstraint may increase local efficiency without increasing system throughput.","r":["Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints | Elevate the System’s Constraints | Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities"],"w":"For Identify Systems Constraints, this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints","d":"What is the best and most accessible mean of eliminating the constraint. Often this requires that we make a quick improvement. Sometimes additional training can help.","g":4,"p":"Exploit the constraint by using its existing capacity as effectively as possible before spending money to expand it.","e":"Example: A warehouse finds that one approval step limits the total flow of order fulfillment. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints: Find the bottleneck, use it fully, align the system, then expand it.","x":"For Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints, improving a nonconstraint may increase local efficiency without increasing system throughput.","r":["Elevate the System’s Constraints | Eleven Steps: Design new System | Identify Systems Constraints"],"w":"For Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints, this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision.","d":"Make sure that the focus is on the most binding constraint. Further other activities and steps in the process must be analyzed to ensure that they are aligned with and support the constraint.","g":4,"p":"Align other activities to support the constraint. Do not allow nonconstraints to overload, starve, or distract the bottleneck.","e":"Example: A software team finds that one approval step limits the total flow of new-feature release. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision.: Find the bottleneck, use it fully, align the system, then expand it.","x":"For Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision., improving a nonconstraint may increase local efficiency without increasing system throughput.","r":["Identify Systems Constraints | Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints | Elevate the System’s Constraints"],"w":"For Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision., this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Elevate the System’s Constraints","d":"If the constraint persists, then further action must be taken to eliminate it. The pressure must continue until the constraint no longer affects process throughput. This may mean adding more machines, replacing suppliers, scheduling more shifts, redesigning web sites, and scheduling more training sessions..","g":4,"p":"If the constraint still limits performance after exploitation and subordination, increase its capacity through investment, redesign, staffing, or technology.","e":"Example: A manufacturer finds that one approval step limits the total flow of assembly process. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Elevate the System’s Constraints: Find the bottleneck, use it fully, align the system, then expand it.","x":"For Elevate the System’s Constraints, improving a nonconstraint may increase local efficiency without increasing system throughput.","r":["Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints | Eleven Steps: Design new System | Identify Systems Constraints"],"w":"For Elevate the System’s Constraints, this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Go Back When Necessary","d":"After one constraint has been addressed another constraint will inevitably affect process throughput. So, in the interest of continuous improvement, it would be appropriate to refocus on the new constraint.","g":4,"p":"Once one constraint is broken, another part of the system becomes the new constraint. Repeat the focusing steps and avoid allowing old policies to become the bottleneck.","e":"Example: A university finds that one approval step limits the total flow of student registration. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Go Back When Necessary: Connect the term to the decision or action it supports.","x":"For Go Back When Necessary, know how the concept is applied, not only how its definition is worded.","r":["Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision. | Elevate the System’s Constraints | Theory of Constraints: Benefits"],"w":"For Go Back When Necessary, this matters because the concept gives the improvement team a clearer way to understand, measure, or improve process performance."},{"t":"Theory of Constraints: Benefits","d":"Less ambiguous to identify what it is that is interfering with achieving process performance goals. Places the focus on exactly where the shortfall in process performance has the most impact on addressing customer needs and achieving organizational profits. Provides a focus for a program of continuous improvement. Takes an overall or systems approach to isolating where backups occur. Encourages using what you already have to mitigate the constraint rather than engaging in costly capital or workforce expenditures such as larger facilities, better equipment or additional personnel.","g":4,"p":"Less ambiguous to identify what it is that is interfering with achieving process performance goals.","e":"Example: A restaurant finds that one approval step limits the total flow of meal preparation. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Theory of Constraints: Benefits: Find the bottleneck, use it fully, align the system, then expand it.","x":"For Theory of Constraints: Benefits, improving a nonconstraint may increase local efficiency without increasing system throughput.","r":["Theory of Constraints | Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps | Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints"],"w":"For Theory of Constraints: Benefits, this matters because improving the system constraint produces more benefit than optimizing work that is not limiting total output."},{"t":"Resistance-to Change","d":"Reluctance or interference by anyone in the organization to changes in process methods and steps.","g":4,"p":"Resistance to change is the hesitation or opposition people may show when a change threatens routines, confidence, status, workload, or trust.","e":"Employees avoid a new workflow because they were not involved and do not understand how it affects their work.","m":"Resistance often signals an unmet need or concern.","x":"Do not assume resistance is simply stubbornness or poor attitude.","r":["Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps | Change Management | Continuous and Disruptive Change"],"w":"For Resistance to Change, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps","d":"Resistance can be reduced by building urgency, forming a coalition, clarifying the vision, communicating, empowering employees, celebrating short-term wins, consolidating gains, and anchoring the change in culture.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps means resistance can be reduced by building urgency, forming a coalition, clarifying the vision, communicating, empowering employees, celebrating short-term wins, consolidating gains, and anchoring the change in culture.","e":"Example: Before changing baggage handling, leaders at a airline explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management | Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"For Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Urgency","d":"A sense of urgency helps. Research has found that without it, few will be motivated to participate in or accept change. Without urgency, there is no compelling reason why staff should accept the changes that are likely to disrupt the way they work and disrupt the pattern of interactions with others in the organization.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using urgency to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing prescription filling, leaders at a pharmacy explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Urgency: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Urgency, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition | Overcoming Resistance: Communicate"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Urgency, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Coalition","d":"Coalitions of stakeholders can also help underscore the need for change. They can be instrumental in providing the momentum necessary to break down the barriers of resistance. The broader the coalition, the more momentum behind the change.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using coalition to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing room turnover, leaders at a hotel explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Coalition: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Coalition, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Communicate | Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Coalition, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Vision and Strategy","d":"Change initiatives must be driven by a strong and clear vision of what will change, how it will improve performance, and how it will prepare the organization for the future.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using vision and strategy to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing package routing, leaders at a delivery company explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Vision and Strategy: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Vision and Strategy, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy | Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Vision and Strategy, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Communicate","d":"Changes must be communicated to all stakeholders. They must not only understand why the changes are necessary and what changes will be made, but also when they will take place and the procedures followed to implement them.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using communicate to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing employee onboarding, leaders at a human-resources team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Communicate: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Communicate, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition | Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Communicate, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Empower Employees","d":"Empowerment occurs when those who are affected by a change are heard by those responsible for making the change. But it also requires that reasonable suggestions are considered during the change process, and some, not necessarily all, are approved.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using empower employees to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing invoice processing, leaders at a finance team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Empower Employees: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Empower Employees, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition | Overcoming Resistance: Communicate"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Empower Employees, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins","d":"It is important for the leader to publicly recognize a series of short-term wins or accomplishments. Most importantly, these wins will serve as opportunities to celebrate progress. In addition, they will go a long way in helping to sustain enthusiasm as the change initiative moves forward.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using short-term wins to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing purchase-order approval, leaders at a purchasing team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Short Term Wins | Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains","d":"As progress continues, it is often useful for the leader to consolidate the gains that have been made and share them with stakeholders. Often, these gains can be used to motivate even more gains.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using consolidate gains to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing patient check-in, leaders at a hospital clinic explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains | Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","d":"When change initiatives are successfully completed, it is important for leaders to “anchor” those successes to the existing culture of the organization. When anchored, the values and beliefs of the organization become stronger and more widely accepted.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using anchor to culture to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing sample processing, leaders at a laboratory explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture: Create readiness, support adoption, reinforce behavior.","x":"For Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture, communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture | Overcoming Resistance: Coalition | Overcoming Resistance: Communicate"],"w":"For Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture, this matters because improvement lasts only when people understand, adopt, and reinforce the new way of working."},{"t":"Teams in name Only","d":"A group of people working together in a conventional or traditional way and under the watchful eye of a team leader or manager is often a team in name only.","g":4,"p":"A team in name only is a group labeled as a team without shared goals, complementary work, mutual accountability, or real collaboration.","e":"Example: A customer-support center forms a cross-functional group to improve ticket resolution. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"Teams in Name Only: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For Teams in Name Only, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"For Teams in Name Only, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams","d":"A ‘real’ Continuous Improvement team, however, which we might refer to as a high-performance team, is a group of people with multiple and complementary skills who are self-directed, strongly motivated to accomplish challenging goals, and committed to improving efficiency while at the same time, also focusing on customer value and customer satisfaction.","g":4,"p":"High-performance teams combine a common purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, disciplined action, and mutual accountability.","e":"A cross-functional improvement team uses shared measures, clear roles, and regular follow-through to reduce defects.","m":"Purpose, goals, skills, discipline, accountability.","x":"A collection of strong individuals is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | Teams in Name Only | High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable"],"w":"For High-Performance Teams, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","d":"High-performance teams share a common purpose, set clear performance goals, combine complementary skills, act with discipline, and hold members mutually accountable.","g":4,"p":"In practical terms, Characteristics of High-Performance Teams means high-performance teams share a common purpose, set clear performance goals, combine complementary skills, act with discipline, and hold members mutually accountable.","e":"Example: A software team forms a cross-functional group to improve new-feature release. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"Characteristics of High-Performance Teams: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For Characteristics of High-Performance Teams, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose | High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills"],"w":"For Characteristics of High-Performance Teams, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Disciplined Action","d":"High-performance Continuous Improvement teams are capable of disciplined action. They are disciplined in establishing a common purpose, establishing performance goals, and holding each other to mutually agreed-upon standards of performance. They are disciplined in following a Democratic style of engagement and following this approach to organize and plan every step in delivering results.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on disciplined action to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A manufacturer forms a cross-functional group to improve assembly process. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"High-Performance Teams: Disciplined Action: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For High-Performance Teams: Disciplined Action, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"For High-Performance Teams: Disciplined Action, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose","d":"Members of the team sharing a common goal. Without it they can work at cross-purposes and outcomes suffer.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on common purpose to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A university forms a cross-functional group to improve student registration. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills"],"w":"For High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Performance Goals","d":"Team decides on its own performance goals.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on performance goals to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A restaurant forms a cross-functional group to improve meal preparation. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"High-Performance Teams: Performance Goals: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For High-Performance Teams: Performance Goals, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"For High-Performance Teams: Performance Goals, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Complimentary SKills","d":"Rather than similar skills, high-performance teams need to include individuals with diverse traits, skills, interests, and backgrounds. It should be a team of people who think differently.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on complementary skills to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A bank forms a cross-functional group to improve loan application. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"For High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable","d":"Holding team members responsible for achieving project goals.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on mutually accountable to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: An airline forms a cross-functional group to improve baggage handling. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable: A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"For High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable, a collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams | High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"For High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable, this matters because shared purpose, clear goals, complementary skills, and accountability allow teams to solve complex problems together."}]);
